# The system's printers: the printers this machine knows listed with their
# state, the papers each takes and what it can do, a PDF document submitted to
# one as a job with the settings a print dialog asks (copies, paper,
# orientation, sides, colour, pages, collation), the job's state and pages
# told while it runs and when it ends, waited for, and cancelled.
#
# The systems: on macOS and Linux CUPS, the print system both ship, its
# library opened at the first call, so a printer is a CUPS queue; on Windows
# the print spooler, the document drawn by the system's own PDF renderer
# (`Windows.Data.Pdf`) through Direct2D's print control into an XPS print job
# the printer's driver receives with a print ticket of the job's settings.
# Nothing else is started: no shell verb, no other program.
#
# What it does NOT do: make the document (`pdf` writes one, text, fonts,
# images and tables, and its bytes are what `submit` takes); show the print
# and page-setup dialogs, which are `ui`'s, answered through its events with
# `Settings`; print a web page, which `ui/webview` does; add, share or remove
# printers, which is the system's settings' work and needs its privileges.
#
# **One document form, PDF, on every system**, so a program that prints on
# one prints on all. **A page larger than the paper is fitted to it**,
# centred, as CUPS fits one; a smaller page is printed at its size. **A job is
# a printer's name and a number**, no handle: it is the system's from the
# moment `submit` answers, and stands after the program ends. **A wait is
# bounded** (`wait`, `FOREVER` for none) and fails `stopped` once the
# program's stop is asked (`os.ask_stop`).
#
# It fails with `invalid`, `not_found`, `permission`, `limit`, `unsupported`,
# `timeout`, `stopped` and `failed` (README § A4).
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: a document in the
# printer's own language sent as it is; the print quality, pages per sheet,
# scaling, finishing and the input tray asked of a job.

use os
use printing/hero_printing
use printing/internal/calls
use printing/internal/checks
use printing/internal/failure
use printing/internal/fields
use printing/internal/trial

## Codes

# An argument refused: a name empty where it may not be, holding a zero byte
# or past `MAX_NAME_BYTES`; bytes that are not a PDF; settings outside their
# values; a wait below `FOREVER`. Every function that takes one fails with
# it.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# No printer of that name, no paper of that name on the printer, no job of
# that number on the printer, or no default printer. Every function that
# names a printer or a job fails with it, and `default_printer` and
# `default_paper`.
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    "not_found"

# The system refuses this to the program: the job is another user's, or the
# print service asks for credentials. Every function that reaches the system
# fails with it.
constant PERMISSION: str
    "permission"

# The system has no memory, or the printer no room for another job. Every
# function that reaches the system fails with it.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

# This machine has no print system the package reaches (Linux with no CUPS
# library, or no scheduler running), or the printer takes no such setting
# (two sides on a printer of one, colour on one of grey). Every function that
# reaches the system fails with it.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

# The job had not ended when the time given ran out. `wait` fails with it.
constant TIMEOUT: str
    "timeout"

# The program's stop was asked while the call waited. `wait` fails with it.
constant STOPPED: str
    "stopped"

# The system refused for a reason none of the above names, a printer that
# takes no job now among them; the message carries the system's words. Every
# function that reaches the system fails with it.
constant FAILED: str
    "failed"

## Limits

# The most bytes a printer's name, a paper's name or a job's title holds:
# IPP's bound on a name.
constant MAX_NAME_BYTES: i64
    255

# The most copies a job asks.
constant MAX_COPIES: i64
    999

# The most page ranges a job names.
constant MAX_RANGES: i64
    100

# No bound on a wait, in milliseconds: `wait` waits until the job ends.
constant FOREVER: i64
    -1

## Printers

# A printer this machine knows.
record Printer
    # The system's name for it, which every other function takes: a CUPS
    # queue's name, the spooler's printer name on Windows.
    name: str
    # The words a person reads for it: CUPS' `printer-info`, the name again
    # on Windows.
    description: str
    # Where it stands, as its owner wrote it; empty where nobody did.
    location: str
    # Its make and model, or its driver's name on Windows.
    model: str
    # Whether it waits, prints, or has stopped.
    state: State
    # Why, as IPP's `printer-state-reasons` keywords (`media-empty`,
    # `offline-report`, `paused`); none where nothing is wrong.
    reasons: [str]
    # The system's own words about its state; empty where it has none.
    message: str
    # Whether it takes jobs now.
    accepting: bool
    # Whether it is this machine's default printer.
    is_default: bool

# What a printer is doing.
variant State
    # Waiting for a job.
    idle
    # Printing, or holding jobs it has not printed.
    processing
    # Stopped: paused by a person, or kept from printing by what `reasons`
    # names.
    stopped

# The printers this machine knows, in the system's order; none where it knows
# none.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where the machine has no print system the package
# reaches, `permission`, `limit` and `failed`. See also `default_printer` and
# `printer_named`.
function printers() -> [Printer]?
    asked = "listing this machine's printers"
    list = calls.read(.printers, asked: asked)?

    match fields.printers_of(list)
        .ok found => return ok(found)
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{asked}: {e.msg}")

# This machine's default printer.
#
# Fails `not_found` where it has none, and as `printers` fails. See also
# `printers`.
function default_printer() -> Printer?
    for p in printers()?
        if p.is_default
            return ok(p)

    return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "this machine has no default printer")

# The printer named `name`.
#
# - `name`: the printer's name, as `printers` lists it.
#
# Fails `invalid` for a name the package refuses, `not_found` where no printer
# is so named, and as `printers` fails. See also `printers`.
function printer_named(name: str) -> Printer?
    checks.name(s: name, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?

    for p in printers()?
        if p.name == name
            return ok(p)

    return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"no printer named \"{name}\"")

## Papers

# A paper a printer takes.
record Paper
    # Its PWG 5101.1 name, the same on every system: `iso_a4_210x297mm`,
    # `na_letter_8.5x11in`; on Windows a paper of no standard size is named
    # `om_dmpaper-<its number>_<w>x<h>mm`. `Settings.paper` takes it.
    name: str
    # The words a person reads for it, the system's own: `A4`, `Letter`.
    label: str
    # Its width upright, in millimetres, more than 0.
    width_mm: f64
    # Its height upright, in millimetres, more than 0.
    height_mm: f64
    # The margin the printer cannot print at the left, in millimetres, 0 or
    # more; 0 for a borderless paper.
    left_mm: f64
    # The margin at the right, in millimetres.
    right_mm: f64
    # The margin at the top, in millimetres.
    top_mm: f64
    # The margin at the bottom, in millimetres.
    bottom_mm: f64
    # Whether it is the printer's default paper.
    is_default: bool

# The papers `printer` takes, in the system's order; none where it names
# none. Asked of the printer's driver or of the printer itself, so it may take
# a second.
#
# - `printer`: the printer's name.
#
# Fails `invalid`, `not_found` where no printer is so named, `unsupported`,
# `permission`, `limit` and `failed`. See also `default_paper`.
function papers(printer: str) -> [Paper]?
    asked = f"reading the papers of printer \"{printer}\""
    checks.name(s: printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?
    list = calls.read(.media(printer: printer), asked: asked)?

    match fields.papers_of(list)
        .ok found => return ok(found)
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{asked}: {e.msg}")

# The paper `printer` prints on where a job names none.
#
# - `printer`: the printer's name.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the printer names no default paper, and as `papers`
# fails. See also `papers`.
function default_paper(printer: str) -> Paper?
    for p in papers(printer)?
        if p.is_default
            return ok(p)

    return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"printer \"{printer}\" names no default paper")

## What a printer can do

# What a printer can do, which `submit` holds a job to.
record Capabilities
    # Whether it prints on both sides of a sheet.
    two_sided: bool
    # Whether it prints colour.
    color: bool
    # The most copies a job may ask of it, 1 to `MAX_COPIES`.
    copies_most: i64

# What `printer` can do. Asked of the printer's driver or of the printer
# itself.
#
# - `printer`: the printer's name.
#
# Fails `invalid`, `not_found` where no printer is so named, `unsupported`,
# `permission`, `limit` and `failed`. See also `submit`.
function capabilities(printer: str) -> Capabilities?
    asked = f"asking what printer \"{printer}\" can do"
    checks.name(s: printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?
    list = calls.read(.capabilities(printer: printer), asked: asked)?

    match fields.capabilities_of(list)
        .ok found => return ok(found)
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{asked}: {e.msg}")

## Settings

# Which way up a page prints on the paper.
variant Orientation
    # Upright.
    portrait
    # Turned a quarter, the long edge across.
    landscape
    # Turned a quarter the other way.
    reverse_landscape
    # Upside down.
    reverse_portrait

# Which sides of a sheet a job prints.
variant Sides
    # One side.
    one_sided
    # Both, turned on the long edge, as a book is.
    two_sided_long_edge
    # Both, turned on the short edge, as a notepad is.
    two_sided_short_edge

# The colour a job prints in.
variant ColorMode
    # What the printer prints by default.
    printer_default
    # Colour.
    color
    # Shades of grey.
    monochrome

# Pages of a document, `first` to `last`, both printed.
record PageRange
    # The first page, from 1.
    first: i64
    # The last page, `first` or after.
    last: i64

# What a job asks of its printer: what a print dialog answers, and what
# `submit` takes. Built directly, or by `default_settings` and changed.
record Settings
    # The printer's name.
    printer: str
    # The copies, 1 to `MAX_COPIES`.
    copies: i64
    # The paper's PWG name, one `papers` lists; empty for the printer's
    # default.
    paper: str
    # Which way up each page prints.
    orientation: Orientation
    # Which sides of a sheet are printed.
    sides: Sides
    # The colour.
    color: ColorMode
    # The pages printed, each range after the one before it and not touching
    # it, at most `MAX_RANGES`; none for every page.
    pages: [PageRange]
    # Whether each copy is printed whole before the next, or each page's
    # copies together.
    collate: bool

# The settings a job on `printer` starts from: one copy, the printer's paper,
# upright, one side, the printer's colour, every page, collated.
#
# - `printer`: the printer's name, not checked here.
#
# See also `submit`.
function default_settings(printer: str) -> Settings
    return Settings(
        printer: printer,
        copies: 1,
        paper: "",
        orientation: .portrait,
        sides: .one_sided,
        color: .printer_default,
        pages: [],
        collate: true
    )

## Jobs

# A job on a printer: what `submit` and `jobs` answer. Built directly by a
# program that kept its two fields; compare two with `==`.
record Job
    # The printer's name.
    printer: str
    # The system's number for the job on that printer, more than 0.
    id: i64

# The state of a job.
variant JobState
    # Waiting for its turn.
    pending
    # Held, by a person or by the system, until released.
    held
    # Printing.
    processing
    # Stopped by its printer, which `reasons` says why; it goes on once the
    # printer does.
    stopped
    # Cancelled, by `cancel` or by a person. An end.
    cancelled
    # Ended by the system for an error. An end.
    aborted
    # Printed whole. An end.
    completed

# How a job goes.
record Status
    # Its state.
    state: JobState
    # Why, as IPP's `job-state-reasons` keywords (`job-printing`,
    # `job-canceled-by-user`); none where the system names none.
    reasons: [str]
    # The system's own words about it; empty where it has none.
    message: str
    # The pages printed so far, IPP's `job-impressions-completed`; 0 where the
    # system counts none.
    pages_printed: i64

# Submits the PDF `pdf` to the printer `settings` names as one job titled
# `title`, and answers it once the system holds the whole document: what is
# printed is the system's from then on, and `status_of` tells how it goes.
#
# - `settings`: what the job asks; its printer must take its paper, its sides
#   and its colour (`capabilities`).
# - `title`: the job's title, as the printer's queue shows it, at most
#   `MAX_NAME_BYTES` bytes; empty for `Untitled`.
# - `pdf`: the document, a PDF, as `pdf.encode` writes one.
#
# Fails `invalid` for settings outside their values, a title past its bound or
# bytes that are not a PDF; `not_found` where the printer or its paper is not
# found; `unsupported` where the printer takes no such sides, colour or copies;
# `permission`, `limit` and `failed`. No job stands after a failure. The
# document is copied once to the system, as it arrives. See also `status_of`,
# `cancel` and `wait`.
function submit(settings: Settings, title: str, pdf: [u8]) -> Job?
    asked = f"submitting \"{title}\" to printer \"{settings.printer}\""
    checks.settings(settings)?
    checks.name(s: title, what: "job's title", may_be_empty: true)?
    document @= pdf
    ranges @= checks.range_numbers(settings.pages)
    collate @= 0

    if settings.collate
        collate @ 1
    got = hero_printing.submit(
        printer: settings.printer,
        title: title,
        @document,
        size: to_u64(document.len()).must(),
        copies: settings.copies,
        media: settings.paper,
        orientation: checks.orientation_number(settings.orientation),
        sides: checks.sides_number(settings.sides),
        color: checks.color_number(settings.color),
        collate: collate,
        @ranges,
        range_count: to_u64(ranges.len()).must()
    )

    if got < 0
        return failure.last(asked)
    return ok(Job(printer: settings.printer, id: got))

# How `job` goes now.
#
# - `job`: the job.
#
# Fails `not_found` where its printer holds no such job (CUPS forgets an
# ended job after a day; Windows' spooler as soon as it ends, the package
# telling then the end this process saw of a job it submitted), `invalid`,
# `unsupported`, `permission` and `failed`. See also `wait` and `is_ended`.
function status_of(job: Job) -> Status?
    asked = f"reading job {job.id} on printer \"{job.printer}\""
    checks.name(s: job.printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?
    list = calls.read(.job(printer: job.printer, id: job.id), asked: asked)?

    match fields.status_of(list)
        .ok found => return ok(found)
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{asked}: {e.msg}")

# Whether `state` is an end: `cancelled`, `aborted` or `completed`.
#
# - `state`: a job's state.
function is_ended(state: JobState) -> bool
    return match state
        .cancelled | .aborted | .completed        => true
        .pending | .held | .processing | .stopped => false

# The jobs on `printer` that have not ended, oldest first: this program's,
# other programs' and other users' as far as the system shows them.
#
# - `printer`: the printer's name.
#
# Fails `invalid`, `not_found` where no printer is so named, `unsupported`,
# `permission` and `failed`. See also `status_of`.
function jobs(printer: str) -> [Job]?
    asked = f"listing the jobs of printer \"{printer}\""
    checks.name(s: printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?
    list = calls.read(.jobs(printer: printer), asked: asked)?

    match fields.jobs_of(list, printer: printer)
        .ok found => return ok(found)
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: f"{asked}: {e.msg}")

# Cancels `job`; a job that had already ended stays as it ended, and is no
# failure.
#
# - `job`: the job.
#
# Fails `not_found` where its printer holds no such job, `permission` where it
# is another user's, `invalid`, `unsupported` and `failed`. See also `submit`.
function cancel(job: Job) -> ()?
    asked = f"cancelling job {job.id} on printer \"{job.printer}\""
    checks.name(s: job.printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?

    if hero_printing.cancel(job.printer, job: job.id) < 0
        return failure.last(asked)
    return ok()

# The milliseconds one wait of the layer takes at most, between which the
# program's stop is asked.
constant SLICE_MS: i64
    250

# Waits at most `within_ms` milliseconds for `job` to end, asking its state
# every quarter of a second, and answers how it ended.
#
# - `job`: the job.
# - `within_ms`: the milliseconds to wait, 0 to ask once and wait not at all,
#   `FOREVER` for no bound.
#
# Fails `timeout` where it had not ended when the time ran out, `stopped`
# once the program's stop is asked, `invalid` for a span below `FOREVER`, and
# as `status_of` fails. See also `status_of`.
function wait(job: Job, within_ms: i64) -> Status?
    asked = f"waiting for job {job.id} on printer \"{job.printer}\""
    checks.name(s: job.printer, what: "printer's name", may_be_empty: false)?

    if within_ms < FOREVER
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"{asked}: a wait of {within_ms} ms, where it is 0 or more, or FOREVER")
    left @= within_ms

    while true
        step_ms @= SLICE_MS

        if within_ms != FOREVER && left < SLICE_MS
            step_ms @ left
        got = hero_printing.wait(job.printer, job: job.id, within_ms: step_ms)

        if got < 0
            return failure.last(asked)

        if got == 1
            return status_of(job)

        if within_ms != FOREVER
            left @ left - step_ms

            if left <= 0
                return fail(code: TIMEOUT, msg: f"{asked}: it had not ended after {within_ms} ms")

        if os.is_stop_asked()
            return fail(code: STOPPED, msg: f"{asked}: the program's stop was asked")

## Examples

# The printers this machine knows, each with its state.
test "printers: each printer's name and state"
    match printers()
        .ok found =>
            for p in found
                assert p.name != "" && p.description != ""
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no print system this program may use: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# The default printer, where the machine has one.
test "default_printer: the one printer marked the default"
    match default_printer()
        .ok p  => assert p.is_default
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no default printer here: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# A printer found by its name.
test "printer_named: the default printer found again by its name"
    match default_printer()
        .ok p  => assert printer_named(p.name).must().name == p.name
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no default printer here: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# The papers of the default printer, its default among them.
test "papers: the default printer's papers, each larger than its margins"
    match default_printer()
        .ok p =>
            for paper in papers(p.name).must()
                assert paper.width_mm > paper.left_mm + paper.right_mm
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no default printer here: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# The paper a job prints on where it names none.
test "default_paper: the default printer's own default paper"
    match default_printer()
        .ok p =>
            match default_paper(p.name)
                .ok paper => assert paper.is_default && paper.name != ""
                .err e    => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no default printer here: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# What a printer can do, asked before a job asks for two sides.
test "capabilities: whether the default printer prints on both sides"
    match default_printer()
        .ok p  => assert capabilities(p.name).must().copies_most >= 1
        .err e => skip_test(reason: f"no default printer here: {e.code}, {e.msg}")

# Settings for two copies on A4, turned on the long edge.
test "default_settings: two copies on A4, both sides"
    s = default_settings("office")
    two = Settings(
        printer: s.printer,
        copies: 2,
        paper: "iso_a4_210x297mm",
        orientation: s.orientation,
        sides: .two_sided_long_edge,
        color: s.color,
        pages: s.pages,
        collate: s.collate
    )
    assert two.copies == 2 && two.printer == "office" && two.pages.len() == 0

# Whether a state is an end.
test "is_ended: completed is an end, processing is not"
    assert is_ended(.completed)
    assert !is_ended(.processing)

# A one-page PDF printed, waited for and its end read; run only where
# `REBEL_PRINT_TEST_PRINTER` names a printer a test may print on, never a
# real one by chance.
test "submit: a one-page PDF, waited for, then cancelled if it had not ended"
    match trial.printer()
        .ok name =>
            job = submit(default_settings(name), title: "rebel printing example", pdf: trial.page()).must()

            match wait(job, within_ms: 2000)
                .ok s => assert is_ended(s.state)
                .err e =>
                    assert e.code == TIMEOUT
                    cancel(job).must()
        .err e => skip_test(reason: e.msg)

# A job's state read once.
test "status_of: a submitted job's state, then cancelled"
    match trial.printer()
        .ok name =>
            job = submit(default_settings(name), title: "rebel printing status", pdf: trial.page()).must()
            assert status_of(job).must().pages_printed >= 0
            cancel(job).must()
        .err e => skip_test(reason: e.msg)

# The jobs waiting on a printer.
test "jobs: a submitted job stands among the printer's jobs until it ends"
    match trial.printer()
        .ok name =>
            job = submit(default_settings(name), title: "rebel printing jobs", pdf: trial.page()).must()
            waiting = jobs(name).must()
            assert waiting.len() >= 0
            cancel(job).must()
        .err e => skip_test(reason: e.msg)

# A job cancelled, and cancelled again once it has ended.
test "cancel: a job cancelled, then cancelled again"
    match trial.printer()
        .ok name =>
            job = submit(default_settings(name), title: "rebel printing cancel", pdf: trial.page()).must()
            cancel(job).must()
            cancel(job).must()
        .err e => skip_test(reason: e.msg)

# A cancelled job waited for: it has ended.
test "wait: a cancelled job ends within a second"
    match trial.printer()
        .ok name =>
            job = submit(default_settings(name), title: "rebel printing wait", pdf: trial.page()).must()
            cancel(job).must()
            assert is_ended(wait(job, within_ms: 1000).must().state)
        .err e => skip_test(reason: e.msg)
